\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\enlargethispage*{4\baselineskip}

\subsection{Temporal DIF as Structured Signal}
\label{sec:disc_temporal}

\looseness=-1
Temporal DIF detects \emph{that} items function differently across cohorts, not \emph{why}---and this is by design. The signal is structured: stratifying by model type (base $N{=}1{,}409$, instruct $N{=}478$, across all 5,227 models) reveals opposite patterns---base models are 60.1\% reference-favoring with a strong difficulty gradient ($\rho = -0.570$), while instruct models are 65.6\% focal-favoring with a near-zero gradient ($\rho = -0.079$; $\chi^2 = 562.7$, $p < 10^{-124}$). Following the impact-bias framework~\citep{camilli1994methods}, this reversal reflects structured item-level bias rather than uniform group impact, validating DIF as a mechanism-agnostic diagnostic (\Cref{app:mechanism_discussion}).

\looseness=-1
The two temporal cohorts differ in base/instruct composition (2023: 79.7\% vs.\ 2024: 67.9\% base). Downsampling to match ratios yields C\% $= 30.25\% \pm 1.32\%$ ($\Delta = 1.08$\,pp; \Cref{app:composition_control}), ruling out composition shift. Within-architecture DIF confirms the signal persists within individual lineages (Llama C\% $= 59.2\%$; \Cref{app:within_arch}), consistent with ability-matched temporal C\% ($28.9\%$).

\subsection{Recommended DIF Audit Protocol}
\label{sec:disc_protocol}

\looseness=-1
Item-level properties do not predict DIF susceptibility (AUROC $= 0.60$; \Cref{app:predictive}), precluding pre-screening. We recommend MH-DIF audits whenever ${\geq}$500 models accumulate ($K{=}3$ for smaller corpora; \Cref{tab:k3_power}), with action thresholds calibrated to $\rho$-degradation (\Cref{app:fig_rho_degradation}). Flagged items warrant mechanism-conditional responses: remove contaminated items, annotate capability-shifted ones, or refine cohort definitions. DIF-corrected scoring motivates equating adjustments~\citep{kolen2014equating,habba2026growingpains}.

\section*{Limitations}

\looseness=-1
Three primary limitations apply. (1)~The semi-synthetic framework uses uniform binary response flipping, a worst-case injection model; reported $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ values are upper-bound estimates, and detection power under realistic contamination patterns (partial memorization, format-specific advantages) is untested. The framework detects \emph{that} items function differently but not \emph{why}---a deliberate design choice that limits immediate actionability; follow-up expert review is needed to determine whether flagged items should be retired or retained. (2)~The MH $\chi^2$ test assumes independent examinees; LLM model families violate this assumption. Three convergent corrections (${\approx}$27--29\%) and family-level cluster bootstrap (95\% CI: $[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$) bound the impact, but the extreme relatedness of some families (e.g., hundreds of Llama-2 fine-tunes) exceeds the clustering levels studied in educational settings~\citep{french2010hierarchical}. (3)~Cross-subject matching assumes contamination affects a minority of domains; $\Delta\mathrm{FPR}$ rises to 0.069 when contamination spans ${\geq}20$ subjects. Although composition control experiments show a small residual shift ($\Delta = 1.08$\,pp; \Cref{app:composition_control}), base/instruct ratio changes between cohorts remain a potential confound that cannot be fully eliminated without single-architecture longitudinal data. Temporal cohorts defined by calendar year provide coarse granularity, and generalizability to open-ended or partial-credit benchmarks is untested. Multidimensional DIF~\citep{ackerman1992didmf} may arise when MMLU's 57 subjects span distinct latent dimensions; cross-subject matching mitigates between-domain multidimensionality but not within-domain violations. IRT parameters are used only for auxiliary power analysis; all core DIF results are nonparametric.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix~\citep{kipnis2024metabench} (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items~\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}), with temporal cohorts defined by release date (2023 vs.\ 2024). Cross-benchmark validation across all six METABENCH benchmarks appears in \Cref{sec:gsm8k}.

\subsection{Validation: DIF Detection Power}
\label{sec:validation}

\looseness=-1
A Monte Carlo power analysis (1,000 replications) using empirical 2PL parameters from PSN-IRT~\citep{zhou2026psnirt} ($\bar{a} = 1.2$, $\bar{b} = -0.84$) confirms that at $N \geq 80$ per cohort, ETS~C achieves TPR of 0.68--0.80 and AUC of 0.93--0.94 for $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.4$ (\Cref{app:method_details}). Our temporal cohorts ($N_{\mathrm{ref}} = 1{,}080$, $N_{\mathrm{focal}} = 807$) far exceed this threshold; power varies with item discrimination (TPR 0.40--0.95 across $a = 0.5$--$2.0$; \Cref{app:power_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Temporal DIF: 31\% of Items Exhibit Differential Functioning}
\label{sec:temporal}

\looseness=-1
We compare 2023 ($N = 1{,}080$) against 2024 ($N = 807$) models; 31.3\% of items receive ETS~C classification ($|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$; bootstrap 95\% CI: $[29.4\%, 38.1\%]$; family-level cluster bootstrap: $[26.7\%, 53.2\%]$, upper bound driven by mega-family over-representation; \Cref{app:uncertainty}). Non-independence corrections---simple-mean DEFF (29.2\%) and cluster bootstrap (26.7\%)---converge on ${\approx}$27--29\%; the conservative weighted-mean DEFF (13.5\%) still exceeds the random baseline by $75\times$ (\Cref{app:deff,app:deff_simulation}). No single family drives the signal (LOFO $|\Delta\mathrm{C\%}| \leq 2.93$\,pp); graduated de-duplication confirms C\% scales with power, not redundancy (\Cref{app:dedup_k}).

\looseness=-1
Three controls confirm genuine temporal change (\Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): random splits yield C\% of 0.18\% (2023) and 1.3\% (2024), both ${\geq}23\times$ below temporal C\%; ability-quintile comparison yields 2.9\%; and 1:1 nearest-neighbor matching on total score ($N{=}638$/group, Cohen's $d = -0.17$) yields C\% $= 28.9\% \pm 0.1\%$, confirming at most ${\sim}2.4$~pp is attributable to ability mismatch ($p < 0.01$; \Cref{app:method_details}).

\looseness=-1
Across all 57 subjects, C\% ranges from 20.7\% to 48.4\% (mean $\approx 30\%$; \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}). DIF rates are domain-invariant (Kruskal-Wallis $p = 0.78$), but DIF \emph{direction} exhibits domain dependence: STEM items favor 2024 models (55.4\%), Humanities favor 2023 (64.4\%). Domain-level accuracy gains explain ${<}0.1\%$ of item-level DIF variance (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$); a Simpson's paradox confirms DIF is associated with item properties rather than aggregate gains (OR reverses from 1.55 to 0.73 after controlling for difficulty; \Cref{app:structural}). A difficulty--direction interaction emerges ($\rho = -0.597$): 2024 models gain on hard items while eroding on easy ones. The conclusion is robust across thresholds ($18.3\%$ at $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 2.0$, $10.2\%$ at $\geq 2.5$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{DIF landscape across cohort definitions. ETS~C denotes the percentage of items with $|\Delta_{\mathrm{MH}}| \geq 1.5$. C\% (web-clean) is the DIF-C flagging rate among items classified as web-clean by \citet{li2024contamination}. Enrichment is the ratio of observed to expected overlap between DIF flags and web-overlap labels. Dashes (--) indicate comparisons where Li et al.\ labels are not applicable.}
\label{tab:temporal_landscape}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Cohort Definition & $N_{\mathrm{ref}}$ / $N_{\mathrm{focal}}$ & C\% & C\% (web-clean) & Enrich. \\
\midrule
Temporal (2023 vs.\ 2024) & 1,080 / 807 & 31.3 & 0.318 & 0.964 \\
Ability quintile (Q1--2 vs.\ Q4--5) & ${\sim}$2,600 / ${\sim}$2,600 & 2.9 & 0.027 & 1.19 \\
Within-family (Llama-2 vs.\ 3) & 161 / 308 & 61.3 & -- & -- \\
Random split 2023 (sanity) & 540 / 540 & 0.18 & -- & -- \\
Random split 2024 (sanity) & ${\sim}$400 / ${\sim}$400 & 1.3 & -- & -- \\
Within-subject (avg, 5 subj.) & \textit{varies} & 38.1 & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Ground Truth Mismatch}
\label{sec:groundtruth}

\looseness=-1
Direct comparison of DIF flags against the web-overlap labels of \citet{li2024contamination} on 9,229 annotated items confirms that the two methods capture orthogonal signals---an empirical verification of the theoretically expected but previously untested distinction between global exposure and differential functioning (\Cref{fig:global_vs_differential}). The enrichment ratio is 0.964 (bootstrap 95\% CI: $[0.94, 1.02]$; \Cref{tab:temporal_landscape}): DIF-C rates are 30.7\% among web-contaminated items and 31.8\% among clean items. Association tests confirm near-independence ($\phi = 0.012$, OR $= 1.05$ [0.96, 1.15], Jaccard $= 0.206$, within permutation null $[0.203, 0.222]$; $p = 0.88$). This near-independence holds across contamination subtypes. The null result does not indicate detection failure: semi-synthetic injection produces a clear dose-response ($\Delta\mathrm{TPR} = {+}0.508$ at $p = 0.5$; \Cref{fig:dose_response}). The disconnect arises because web-overlap labels measure \emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists in training corpora), while DIF measures \emph{differential functioning} (whether an item's relationship to ability changes across cohorts). The two constructs---global exposure and differential functioning---are empirically orthogonal.
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_2_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Semi-synthetic dose-response for DIF detection on MMLU. $\Delta\mathrm{TPR}$ increases monotonically with exploitation rate $p$, confirming that MH-DIF is sensitive to differential item behavior when present. Baseline (no injection) produces $\Delta\mathrm{TPR} \approx 0$.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Structural Analysis}
\label{sec:structural}

\looseness=-1
Two structural controls and a sensitivity check fail to reduce temporal C\% below 31\% (\Cref{tab:structural_comparison}): matched-ability quintiles, within-subject DIF (C\% $= 38.1\%$; cross-subject C\% is more conservative due to lower local dependence), and IRT $\theta$-conditioning ($r(\theta, \mathrm{total\ score}) = 0.9986$). Logistic regression detects an additional 17.7\% with non-uniform DIF ($|\beta_{\mathrm{interaction}}| \geq 0.5$), reaching a union of 47\% (\Cref{app:structural}). Ability-matched C\% converges to ${\sim}$28--29\% for both temporal and within-family comparisons despite vastly different unmatched rates (\Cref{app:structural}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Structural controls and sensitivity checks for temporal DIF. None reduces C\% below 31\%; $\theta$-conditioning serves as a sensitivity check confirming total-score adequacy.}
\label{tab:structural_comparison}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Approach & Mechanism & Temp.\ C\% & TPR ($p{=}0.5$) & Outcome \\
\midrule
